592186049949 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049949 #edb/ref 1031 "Interior Angles of Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049949 #edb/ref 1033 496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049949 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "209a2a6e-0f67-50a5-9c80-5938225e61aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1031 "Exterior Angles of Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1033 6461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050477 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4cbb1cf0-437d-55f7-bb1a-f0140715feb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1031 "Exterior Angles of Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1033 1321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050650 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5c5917ee-8504-5c4f-8c53-d54f4a7e936f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110400 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110400 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110400 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110400 #edb/ref 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110401 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110401 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $x$ in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6e/6eb087290fa6ea66325d35fa626ef385bb4d4e477e6e1c40057b9f9d7e43d9d4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110401 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9bf29fd-5151-591f-b6af-8f5e53b3d231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110401 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{145}^{∘}+x+(2x-{25}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {120}^{∘}+3x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={240}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110402 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110402 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ,$ respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e97d1ab9-f0b2-5e27-afff-a84da69c7487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110402 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\n  \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{120}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {220}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m\\angle C = 40^\\circ.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110771 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110772 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110401 #edb/ref 175