#edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505720 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505720 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505720 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505720 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec105581-d8ab-5cfb-9904-990edbb43675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3bfe01c879d52b6b0cb6dbde604b506a173885ffad258cd9fa52ec298d579201.png)\n\nFind the value of $x$ in the figure above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4267ffed-6129-5b8f-a3a0-b5586fcb6f27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505761 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{80}^{∘}+(x-{20}^{∘})+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{60}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={300}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={150}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8bd85835b1bb951df5773824b45f53527a4cda3c4e988f7b30f151e289d47459.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, we know that \n$m∠A=m∠B.$\nWhat is the measure of the angle $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "582dce9c-b3c2-5822-8e23-d7a58cdba267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505768 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the measure of the interior angle at $A.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=x.\n$$\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{110}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{110}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={70}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+x+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={55}^{∘}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =x+x \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {55}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${119}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $C$ is ${103}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $B?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a5dab59-2250-5c49-b3b0-a8075568ace9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505783 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/6f/6fc411e7715044257a6818fe63fb3b7bd212c22a2f3d279f282b2b7161be3ecc.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $B$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{119}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠B={42}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where the measure of an exterior angle at $C$ is equal to ${127}^{∘}$ and $m∠B={73}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the angle $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a9f9e86-5147-5e3f-981f-c02eff224304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505791 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/07/0729bbdc2bec04cee7be1f0be31a44648b05a2536f7e17002d1dab6e2f3500c6.png)\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{127}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{127}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={53}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $∠A$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{73}^{∘}+{53}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{126}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $m∠A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {127}^{∘} & =m∠A+{73}^{∘} \\\\ {127}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} & =m∠A \\\\ {54}^{∘} & =m∠A\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/07/07059a0b58535952d244533e58d513f4bf538b1e2f4237888f13cc4751dab893.png)\n\nIn the figure above, the value of $x$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0fca551-3459-553b-ab09-61f3dc19f41b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505819 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+(14x+{2}^{∘})+(12x+{18}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x+{20}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x & ={340}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${100}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $B$ is ${122}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0d1d413-28f9-5b6c-8380-80f5141c57c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505838 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/b1/b1d04d90855560e36762d492f34ac14fb960c98f55c234da7c706e638fd9ea9e.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{100}^{∘}+{122}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠C={42}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
